<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>You're not really being scientific or clinical with this document. For instance, how, exactly, do we know that these things are sapient? How do we even know that they are entities if there's no way to see/measure them? It sounds like you're taking the word of the people &quot;possessed,&quot; in which case it could very well be memetic.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Researchers have direct experience of being infested by SCP-3119, as stated late on in the text. I'll clarify by adding a note earlier on, in the section you identified.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>We only know of two instances, but you claim that during a &quot;New Yorker&quot; event, they will split in two. So I guess this only happened once, and we're assuming it happens every time? Then you claim the instances &quot;accelerate&quot; away from -C. Like how do we know where they're going, what they're doing if we can't see or track them? How do we even know where they went, or what they did?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a definite weakness in the text, and one I had consciously decided to let stand for reasons of length. SCP-3119 is very easy to detect and monitor using &lt;whatever the standard telepathic methods are&gt;. I don't really want to describe the standard methods, and referring to them added to length without serving an obvious purpose. Since you've called it out, I'll clarify in the text that this is the case.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Yeah, no, this really needs to be at least Euclid. Things far easier to contain are Euclid.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See above. It's well understood, friendly, and reliably contained.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What does that even mean? [snip]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>SCP-3119-C was on fire, so she led a conga line into and then out of the building to pick up SCP-3119. I'll clarify to &quot;into a burning building to retrieve SCP-3119&quot;.</p>
<p>Thanks again!</p>
