			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3635728">
									

<p>The thing is, there's a guideline called the &quot;locked box test&quot;. 9 times out if 10, it's an accurate indicator for what your object class should be.</p>
<p>The Locked Box Test is more or less as follows:</p>
<ul>
<li>If you lock it in a box and it stays there, it's Safe.</li>
<li>If you lock it in a box and need to check up on every so often it to make sure it's still there, it's Euclid.</li>
<li>If you lock it in a box, and the only thing keeping it in the box is twine and prayer, it's Keter.</li>
<li>If it <em>is</em> the box, it's Thaumiel.</li>
</ul>
<p>Strictly speaking, the Foundation is <em>required</em> to actively maintain two nightclubs and make sure they're popular venues, <em>or the SCP breaches containment and they gotta set up or buy out more nightclubs</em>. They can't forget about the SCP and assume it'll sit there indefinitely, they need to be <em>actively</em> working on its containment to make sure it stays put.</p>
<p>This is a solid Euclid, no two ways about it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I want to tell an everyday story in the SCP universe, so that means I want 3119 to be Safe.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then the choices here are to either scale back the effects of the SCP so it is no longer Euclid, or accept the fact that people are gonna say &quot;this isn't Safe&quot; and give downvotes as a result.</p>

							</div>

								
