														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3635730">
									

<p>Promising start for a new writer. I'm afraid I downvoted based mainly on the lapses in clinical tone. Use of &quot;we&quot; was surprisingly refreshing, and it's an innovation I'd like to see used elsewhere. Likewise with the generally didactic (as in the sense of &quot;instructive&quot;/&quot;educational&quot;) manner - e.g. &quot;which we define here as&#8230;&quot; - which both aids the real-life reader's comprehension and, in-universe, fits canonically with the idea of these pages being introductory containment manuals. However, combined with passages like &quot;music seems purer&#8230; radiate <em>storge</em>&quot;, these represent too large a break from the existing unwritten style guide. I tend to be quite conservative with this sort of thing, I'm afraid.</p>
<p>Speaking to the narrative: the entities are meant to be broadly sympathetic, and that's conveyed effectively, but I couldn't truly feel them. They're incorporeal emotionally as well as physically, somehow. The passage at the end with the &quot;polite smile&quot;/&quot;blank stare&quot; was a real highlight, and came closest to giving us a glimpse into their motivations; a little more description of their reactions like that would have helped bring them to life. I can't help but wonder if they'd have felt more real if they had some kind of actual projected avatar – if the person whose mind was being occupied experienced some distinct image or persona that they could recount to us in the document.</p>
<p>But I'm looking forward to seeing you write more here. You've got a distinctive approach to scip-writing that I want to see more of.</p>

							</div>

								
					
